/F2 18 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 724 Tm (Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues: A Short Primer) Tj
/F2 14 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 692 Tm (Vectors that keep their direction) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 677 Tm (Most vectors change direction when a matrix multiplies them. A matrix can rotate,) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 662 Tm (shear, or stretch space, and a typical vector ends up pointing somewhere new. A) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 647 Tm (few special vectors are different. When the matrix acts on them, they stay on the) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 632 Tm (same line through the origin and are only stretched or shrunk.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 617 Tm (These special vectors are called eigenvectors. The amount of stretching is a) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 602 Tm (number called the eigenvalue. If the eigenvalue is 2, the eigenvector doubles in) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 587 Tm (length. If it is one half, the vector shrinks to half its length. If it is) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 572 Tm (negative, the vector flips to point the other way along the same line.) Tj
/F2 14 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 540 Tm (The defining equation) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 525 Tm (The whole idea fits in one equation: A x equals lambda x. Here A is the matrix, x) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 510 Tm (is the eigenvector, and lambda is the eigenvalue. The equation says that) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 495 Tm (multiplying x by the matrix gives the same result as multiplying x by a single) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 480 Tm (number.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 465 Tm (Because x must not be the zero vector, the equation only has solutions for special) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 450 Tm (values of lambda. Rewriting it as \(A minus lambda I\) x equals zero shows why: the) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 435 Tm (matrix A minus lambda I must squash some direction to zero, so its determinant) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 420 Tm (must be zero.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 405 Tm (- The characteristic equation is det\(A minus lambda I\) equals 0.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 390 Tm (- Its roots are the eigenvalues.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 375 Tm (- For each eigenvalue, the eigenvectors fill a line or plane called the eigenspace.) Tj
